# Village weaver

The village weaver plaits the best known of all suspended chambers, and a single
tree in sub-Saharan Africa may carry two hundred of them swinging from the
branch tips. The male tears long fibres from palm fronds and stems and twines
strip against strip around a drooping twig until the fabric holds, then works it
into a roofed, kidney-shaped chamber for the eggs, entered from underneath and
with nothing supporting it from below. He may finish a dozen in a season,
displaying beneath each as a female arrives to tug at the plaiting. The breeding
male is black-faced and bright yellow. See [[ploceidae]].
